8. COMMAND LIFECYCLE
Every command follows:
RECEIVED
 ↓
AUTHORIZING
 ↓
VALIDATING
 ↓
PROPOSED
 ↓
EXECUTING
 ↓
COMMITTING
 ↓
COMPLETED
Failure states:
REJECTED
FAILED
CANCELLED
STALE
CONFLICT
A command may not transition directly from:
RECEIVED
to:
COMPLETED
without validation.

9. REVISION CONCURRENCY
Every mutating request contains an expected revision.
Example:
expected_revision = 17
The service compares this against the project's current revision.
If:
current_revision != expected_revision
the command becomes:
STALE
The system does not silently overwrite the newer project state.
This provides optimistic concurrency control.

10. TRANSACTION SERVICE
The transaction service is responsible for atomic project-state changes.
Conceptually:
BEGIN
 ↓
Load project
 ↓
Load current revision
 ↓
Verify expected revision
 ↓
Authorize actor
 ↓
Load affected objects
 ↓
Validate command
 ↓
Validate constraints
 ↓
Apply mutation
 ↓
Create model version
 ↓
Create revision
 ↓
Create change records
 ↓
Create event
 ↓
COMMIT
Any failure causes:
ROLLBACK

11. DOMAIN VALIDATION
Domain validation occurs before persistence.
Examples:
level elevation cannot conflict with project level ordering
space cannot have invalid boundary state
element host must exist
relationship endpoints must exist
element cannot reference nonexistent assembly
constraint target must exist
revision must belong to project
command target must belong to project
Domain validation is independent from building-code validation.

12. REGULATORY VALIDATION SEPARATION
OneDraft shall maintain a strict distinction between:
domain validity
and:
regulatory compliance.
For example:
A wall may be geometrically valid
while:
the proposed wall assembly may fail a code requirement.
Therefore:
DOMAIN VALIDATION
and:
COMPLIANCE VALIDATION
are separate execution stages.

13. ARIA ORCHESTRATION
Aria is the intelligence layer responsible for translating natural-language project instructions into structured OneDraft operations.
The execution chain becomes:
USER
 ↓
ARIA
 ↓
INTENT INTERPRETATION
 ↓
TARGET IDENTIFICATION
 ↓
COMMAND PROPOSAL
 ↓
CONSTRAINT ANALYSIS
 ↓
DOMAIN VALIDATION
 ↓
COMMAND EXECUTION
Aria does not directly modify PostgreSQL.

14. ARIA PROVIDER ADAPTER
The AI infrastructure shall use an adapter boundary:
AriaOrchestrator
        ↓
AIProviderAdapter
        ↓
Model Provider
The domain application does not depend on the identity of the underlying model provider.
This preserves provider independence.

15. ARIA REQUEST PIPELINE
An Aria request proceeds through:
NATURAL LANGUAGE
 ↓
CONTEXT ASSEMBLY
 ↓
MODEL INTERPRETATION
 ↓
STRUCTURED INTENT
 ↓
TARGET RESOLUTION
 ↓
COMMAND GENERATION
 ↓
COMMAND VALIDATION
 ↓
EXECUTION
The AI response itself is not considered authoritative project state.
Only the validated command result becomes project state.

16. ARIA CONTEXT
Aria receives only the project context required for the operation.
Context may include:
project metadata
current revision
model version
affected objects
relationships
constraints
requirements
relevant code manifest
previous redlines
drawing context
validation results
The entire database must not be indiscriminately transmitted to the model.

17. ARIA OUTPUT CONTRACT
Aria should produce structured output.
Conceptually:
{
  "intent": "MOVE_ELEMENT",
  "targets": [
    "uuid"
  ],
  "parameters": {
    "distance": 300,
    "unit": "mm",
    "direction": "NORTH"
  },
  "preserve_constraints": [
    "uuid"
  ],
  "confidence": 0.96
}
The application layer converts this into a domain command.

18. AI CONFIDENCE
AI confidence is informational.
Confidence alone must never authorize an unsafe model mutation.
The authoritative sequence remains:
AI INTERPRETATION
 ↓
DOMAIN VALIDATION
 ↓
CONSTRAINT VALIDATION
 ↓
EXECUTION

19. DESIGN PROPOSAL MODE
For complex changes, Aria may produce a design proposal rather than immediately executing a command.
Example:
USER REQUEST
 ↓
ARIA
 ↓
DESIGN PROPOSAL
 ↓
IMPACT ANALYSIS
 ↓
USER / SYSTEM APPROVAL
 ↓
COMMAND
 ↓
EXECUTION
This is required where multiple dependent objects may be affected.

20. IMPACT ANALYSIS
Before executing a complex command, the system determines:
affected elements
affected spaces
affected levels
affected relationships
affected constraints
affected views
affected drawings
affected schedules
affected validation rules
The result becomes an impact graph.

21. DEPENDENCY GRAPH
The existing:
model.relationships
table forms the persistence layer for dependency relationships.
The runtime builds an execution graph:
OBJECT
 ↓
DEPENDENCIES
 ↓
DERIVED OBJECTS
 ↓
DOCUMENTATION
 ↓
VALIDATION
This allows OneDraft to determine which derived artifacts require regeneration.

22. DIRTY STATE
Objects affected by a model change become dirty.
Conceptually:
CLEAN
DIRTY
REGENERATING
VALID
INVALID
A wall modification may mark:
wall
space
plan view
section view
elevation
dimension
schedule
validation result
drawing
document package
as requiring regeneration or revalidation.

